+## 🧪 Testing & CI
+
+### Running the test suite
+The suite runs fully **offline** — no Neo4j instance, Docker container, or `GROQ_API_KEY` is required. Neo4j calls are mocked/patched in the test modules; only the deterministic, non-network code paths are exercised.
+
+```bash
+pip install -r requirements.txt   # or the minimal set below
+pytest -q
+```
+
+If you don't want the full app dependency set (torch, streamlit, faiss, …) just to run tests, install only what the suite imports:
+```bash
+pip install neo4j python-dotenv pyyaml fastmcp apscheduler pytest pytest-cov
+pytest -q
+```
+
+With coverage (matches what CI checks — scoped to the modules under test, gated at 40%):
+```bash
+pytest -q --cov=agents.automation_engine --cov=agents.ontology_engine --cov=mcp_server \
+  --cov-report=term-missing --cov-fail-under=40
+```
+
+Current suite: `tests/test_automation_engine.py`, `tests/test_ontology_engine.py`, `tests/test_mcp_server.py`.
+
+### Linting
+Config lives in `pyproject.toml` (`ruff` + `black`, line length 100). The repo has ~167 pre-existing findings (mostly whitespace) that haven't been auto-fixed yet, so the lint CI job runs with `continue-on-error: true` for now — it reports but doesn't block merges. To clean it up (do this as its own isolated commit, separate from feature work):
+```bash
+pip install ruff black
+ruff check --fix .
+black .
+```
+Once that's committed, remove `continue-on-error: true` from `.github/workflows/ci.yml` so lint actually gates PRs.
+
+### CI
+`.github/workflows/ci.yml` runs on every push and pull request:
+- **lint** — `ruff check .` + `black --check .` (non-blocking until the codebase is reformatted, see above)
+- **audit** — `pip-audit` against `requirements.lock.txt`
+- **test** — `pytest` with the 40% coverage gate described above
+
+`.github/dependabot.yml` opens weekly PRs for outdated pip and GitHub Actions dependencies.
+
+### Dependency locking
+`requirements.txt` now caps every dependency below its next major version, so a plain install can't silently jump onto a breaking release. `requirements.lock.txt` pins exact versions for a reproducible install — regenerate it after any deliberate version bump:
+```bash
+python -m venv .lockenv && .lockenv/bin/pip install -r requirements.txt
+.lockenv/bin/pip freeze > requirements.lock.txt
+```
 
